# Q: does the campaign's briefing play — the set, the man, the light, the camera and the words?
#
#   MiVic.Game.exe --cutscene m1_briefing --probe tools/probe/cutscene.probe --probe-out artifacts/probe/cutscene.txt
#
# The scene, driven without a viewer. `cutscene state` reports what is standing in the room as
# model and part counts, which is how a scene that renders an empty frame is caught as a number
# rather than as a photograph somebody has to look at — the first version of this scene drew the
# man and an invisible room, because the set's part transforms were never filled in.
#
# The clock is the probe's, not the wall clock's: a probe process drives the director explicitly,
# so two runs of this script advance the scene by exactly the same amount and the two shots are
# the same two moments.
#
# The checks are the two failures a transcript can hold: a scene with nothing to draw, and a
# scene that finished without saying everything it was written to say.

cutscene state
shot artifacts/probe/cutscene-open.png

# Line one, and the frame it is read in.
cutscene advance 1200
cutscene state

# The camera's first move, and the second line.
cutscene advance 4200
cutscene state
shot artifacts/probe/cutscene-line2.png

# The end of the script: six more lines' worth of time than there is script left.
cutscene advance 30000
cutscene state

# Skipping is not the same road as waiting, so it is asked separately: every line must still be
# counted as said, because a viewer who skips has still been told.
cutscene restart
cutscene state
cutscene skip
cutscene state

# Seeking is how a scene is photographed frame by frame: the camera track, the line being read
# and the lines already said all come back out of the script, so a frame is a function of a
# moment rather than of how long somebody waited.
cutscene seek 11500
cutscene state
shot artifacts/probe/cutscene-line3.png
